about=heading('The studio / Common Ground','A practice of<br>paying attention.','We believe a good building makes everyday life feel a little easier. It holds the light, welcomes change, and belongs to its surroundings.')+f'''<div class=\"wrap studio-layout\">{photo('detail','portrait-photo',True,True)}<div class=\"studio-copy\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">Thoughtful by nature</span><h2>Start with what<br>is already good.</h2><p>A well-placed window. A wall with a useful history. A garden that catches the afternoon sun. These are the starting points for our work.</p><p>Common Ground is an imagined independent practice for homeowners and small cultural organizations. Our approach brings the same care to a kitchen threshold as to a shared reading room: understand the daily routines, ask what matters, and give each decision a clear purpose.</p><p>We favor direct conversations, legible drawings, and materials that can be maintained. The ambition is quiet: places that earn their keep, year after year.</p>{link('services_preview.html','How we work with you')}</div></div>
<section class=\"section wash\"><div class=\"wrap\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">Our principles</span><h2>Three things we return to.</h2><div class=\"principles\"><article><span class=\"eyebrow\">01 / Enough</span><h3>Make space count.</h3><p>Before adding floor area, look at how the existing rooms connect. A simpler route or a shared threshold can unlock more than an extension.</p></article><article><span class=\"eyebrow\">02 / Endurance</span><h3>Think beyond the finish.</h3><p>Choose for touch, repair, and daily wear. A material should look at home now and remain useful as lives and needs change.</p></article><article><span class=\"eyebrow\">03 / Belonging</span><h3>Look beyond the boundary.</h3><p>Consider neighbors, streets, and shared edges. Even a modest project can make its little part of the world more welcoming.</p></article></div></div></section>
<section class=\"wrap section\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">Working together</span><h2>A clear path, taken together.</h2><ol class=\"process-list\"><li><span class=\"number\">01</span><h3>Listen closely</h3><p>We discuss what works, what gets in the way, and what you can realistically invest. Your brief begins with everyday life.</p></li><li><span class=\"number\">02</span><h3>Explore carefully</h3><p>We compare a small number of meaningful options, explaining the tradeoffs in space, cost, light, and complexity.</p></li><li><span class=\"number\">03</span><h3>Resolve together</h3><p>We develop the preferred direction in stages, with clear decisions and agreed responsibilities before moving forward.</p></li></ol></section>'''
page('about-us_preview.html','The studio','The principles behind Common Ground: enough space, enduring materials, and architecture that belongs.',about)
services=heading('Services / Places for everyday life','Considered from<br>the very beginning.','Three areas of practice, connected by a simple aim: make useful, enduring places without doing more than the project needs.',True)+f'''<div class=\"wrap space-bottom\"><section class=\"offering\" id=\"renovations\"><span class=\"number\">01</span><div><h2>Renovations<br>&amp; additions</h2><p class=\"service-sub\">A better fit for the life you live now.</p>{link('single_services_preview.html','Our renovation approach')}</div><div><p>For homes with good bones and rooms that no longer quite work. We explore what to keep, what to connect, and where a measured addition could make a difference.</p><ul><li>Existing layout and daylight studies</li><li>Kitchen, living, and circulation changes</li><li>Careful additions and material reuse</li><li>Phased design and construction information</li></ul></div></section><section class=\"offering\" id=\"compact-homes\"><span class=\"number\">02</span><div><h2>Compact<br>new homes</h2><p class=\"service-sub\">A modest footprint. A generous way of living.</p>{link('work_preview.html#little-gable','See Little Gable')}</div><div><p>For small sites and people who would rather build well than build big. We begin with orientation, privacy, and the rituals of a normal day, then bring structure and services into a straightforward plan.</p><ul><li>Site opportunities and feasibility</li><li>Efficient layouts and flexible rooms</li><li>Daylight, shading, and outdoor connections</li><li>Material and building-system coordination</li></ul></div></section><section class=\"offering\" id=\"civic-interiors\"><span class=\"number\">03</span><div><h2>Civic<br>interiors</h2><p class=\"service-sub\">Small spaces with a shared purpose.</p>{link('work_preview.html#the-reading-room','See The Reading Room')}</div><div><p>For independent libraries, cultural groups, and neighborhood organizations. We help make existing rooms welcoming and adaptable, balancing public use with storage, upkeep, and the people who run the place.</p><ul><li>Conversations with users and organizers</li><li>Access, circulation, and flexible layouts</li><li>Joinery, lighting, and acoustic intent</li><li>Durable finishes and phased improvements</li></ul></div></section></div>
<section class=\"section wash\"><div class=\"wrap faq-layout\"><div><span class=\"eyebrow\">Before we begin</span><h2>A few useful<br>questions.</h2><p class=\"fine-print\">Every project starts with its own circumstances.</p></div><div>{faq([('When should I speak to an architect?','As soon as you have a question about a site or an existing building. Early conversations can help define the brief before you commit to a layout, purchase, or construction schedule.'),('Do I need a finished brief?','No. A few photographs, a rough budget range, and a list of what is and is not working are a useful beginning. We would shape a clearer brief together.'),('How would fees be agreed?','After an initial discussion, the scope, stages, deliverables, and exclusions would be set out in a written proposal. Specialist advice and statutory fees would be identified separately; this sample does not quote fees.'),('Can the work happen in stages?','Yes, when the building and scope allow it. We would plan the whole first, then consider sensible phases so that early work does not prevent later improvements.'),('Are these completed projects?','No. Common Ground and the three portfolio projects are fictional. Photographs are approved architectural references, not images of built work by this practice.')])}</div></div></section>'''
page('services_preview.html','Services','Renovations, compact new homes, and civic interiors: explore the Common Ground approach and frequently asked questions.',services)
print('Created studio and services pages.')

