work=heading('Work / Three fictional design studies','Different places.<br>Common ground.','A home opened to its garden. A small house with room to adapt. A welcoming room for a neighborhood. Three imagined briefs, explored with the same care.')+f'''<div class=\"wrap\"><nav class=\"jump-nav\" aria-label=\"Project index\"><a href=\"#courtyard-house\">01 / Courtyard House ↓</a><a href=\"#little-gable\">02 / Little Gable ↓</a><a href=\"#the-reading-room\">03 / The Reading Room ↓</a></nav><p class=\"fine-print\">All projects are fictional proposals. Photographs illustrate architectural themes and do not depict these projects.</p>
<article class=\"project\" id=\"courtyard-house\"><div class=\"project-head\"><div><span class=\"eyebrow accent\">01 / Renovation study</span><h2>Courtyard House</h2></div><p>A familiar home, rearranged around a pocket of daylight. The proposal finds more room in the plan before asking more of the site.</p></div>{photo('detail')}<div class=\"project-notes\"><dl class=\"project-meta\"><div><dt>Setting</dt><dd>A quiet inner suburb</dd></div><div><dt>Brief</dt><dd>Renovation with a small rear addition</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Fictional concept</dd></div></dl><div><h3>The question</h3><p>How could a deep, narrow house feel connected to its garden without replacing the spaces that give it character? The imagined owners wanted a shared kitchen and a quiet place to work.</p></div><div><h3>The response</h3><p>Retain the front rooms, bring circulation beside a small courtyard, and gather cooking and eating beneath one simple roof. Existing doors and sound timber would be surveyed for reuse.</p>{link('single_services_preview.html','Explore the renovation process')}</div></div></article>
<article class=\"project project-aside\" id=\"little-gable\"><div><span class=\"eyebrow accent\">02 / Compact home study</span><h2>Little Gable</h2><p>A new home for a small plot at a village edge. The concept replaces spare corridors with connected rooms and uses a sheltered threshold to extend everyday life outdoors.</p><p>A flexible ground-floor room could serve as a workspace, guest room, or future bedroom. A central service zone keeps the remaining plan clear, while deep window reveals offer places to sit and pause.</p><p>The material idea is deliberately limited: a durable outer layer, a straightforward timber interior, and parts that can be reached for repair.</p><dl class=\"project-meta\"><div><dt>Setting</dt><dd>A village edge</dd></div><div><dt>Brief</dt><dd>A small, adaptable new home</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Fictional concept</dd></div><div><dt>Guiding idea</dt><dd>Less circulation, more living</dd></div></dl>{link('services_preview.html#compact-homes','About compact homes')}</div>{photo('hero','portrait-photo')}</article>
<article class=\"project\" id=\"the-reading-room\"><div class=\"project-dark\"><div class=\"project-head\"><div><span class=\"eyebrow\">03 / Civic interior study</span><h2>The Reading Room</h2></div><p>An ordinary shopfront becomes an open invitation: a place to read, meet a neighbor, or stay a little longer.</p></div>{photo('hero','landscape-photo',False)}<p class=\"dark-caption\">Illustrative architectural reference · Pierre Châtel-Innocenti / Unsplash</p><div class=\"project-notes\"><dl class=\"project-meta\"><div><dt>Setting</dt><dd>A neighborhood high street</dd></div><div><dt>Brief</dt><dd>A shared reading and meeting space</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Fictional concept</dd></div></dl><div><h3>A room that can change</h3><p>Low shelves would define quieter edges while movable tables keep the center open. A reading session in the morning could give way to a workshop in the afternoon.</p></div><div><h3>Care behind the scenes</h3><p>The proposal reserves proper space for storage, a clear entrance, and an easy-to-find welcome desk. Acoustic finishes and controllable light support different ways of using the room.</p></div></div></div></article></div>'''
page('work_preview.html','Selected studies','Three fictional architectural studies: Courtyard House, Little Gable, and The Reading Room.',work)
journal=heading('Journal / Notes from the studio','Things worth<br>looking at closely.','Short observations on light, materials, and making good use of what we have.',True)+f'''<div class=\"wrap space-bottom\"><div class=\"journal-feature\">{photo('detail','',True,True)}<div><span class=\"eyebrow accent\">Field note 01 / Light</span><h2>Before adding glass,<br>follow the light.</h2><p>A brighter room begins with observation. The size of the window is only part of the story.</p>{link('#light','Read the field note')}</div></div><nav class=\"jump-nav\" aria-label=\"Journal articles\"><a href=\"#light\">01 / Following the light ↓</a><a href=\"#materials\">02 / Materials that age well ↓</a><a href=\"#reuse\">03 / Begin with what remains ↓</a></nav>
<article class=\"journal-article\" id=\"light\"><div class=\"article-meta\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">01 / Light</span><span>Studio notes · 2 min read</span></div><div class=\"article-body\"><h2>Before adding glass,<br>follow the light.</h2><p>A room can have a large window and still feel dim. The depth of the plan, the direction it faces, and the surfaces opposite the opening all influence how light travels. Start by noticing where you actually spend time, rather than assuming that more glazing is always the answer.</p><h3>Watch an ordinary day</h3><p>Make a simple plan and mark the pools of light in the morning, at midday, and in the late afternoon. Repeat in different seasons if you can. Note where you turn on a lamp, where glare makes a screen difficult to read, and which rooms become uncomfortable in strong sun.</p><h3>Think about the path, not just the opening</h3><p>A lighter ceiling or a wider internal opening may let borrowed light reach the center of a home. Moving a tall cupboard can change the way a room feels without changing a wall. Where new glazing makes sense, consider shade, privacy, views, and heat together.</p><div class=\"takeaway\"><strong>A useful first step</strong><p>Photograph the same room from the same position at three times of day. Bring those observations into your first design conversation.</p></div></div></article>
<article class=\"journal-article\" id=\"materials\"><div class=\"article-meta\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">02 / Materials</span><span>Studio notes · 2 min read</span></div><div class=\"article-body\"><h2>Choose for the years<br>after the photograph.</h2><p>A material sample is usually small, clean, and perfectly lit. Daily life is less controlled. Shoes bring in grit, chairs move, and water gathers near a sink. A useful material choice accounts for those ordinary moments, as well as the impression on the first day.</p><h3>Ask how it will be maintained</h3><p>Can the surface be cleaned with readily available products? Can a damaged section be replaced without removing the whole assembly? Some finishes invite local repair; others rely on an unbroken coating. Neither is automatically right, but the upkeep should fit the way you want to live.</p><h3>Put the samples together</h3><p>Look at the floor beside the joinery, the wall finish beside the window frame. View them in the actual room and under evening light. A restrained palette often allows texture and proportion to do more of the work, and can make future repairs easier to integrate.</p><div class=\"takeaway\"><strong>A useful first step</strong><p>Ask for maintenance information with each sample. Compare the work needed over time, not only the purchase price.</p></div></div></article>
<article class=\"journal-article\" id=\"reuse\"><div class=\"article-meta\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">03 / Reuse</span><span>Studio notes · 2 min read</span></div><div class=\"article-body\"><h2>Begin with<br>what remains.</h2><p>The first design move in a renovation can be a list of things to keep. That list might include the structure, but also an old door, a useful shelf, or the way two rooms connect. Looking carefully helps separate a real problem from something that simply looks unfamiliar.</p><h3>Make a keep, adapt, replace inventory</h3><p>Walk through the building before selecting new finishes. Record each element, its condition, and why it matters. A door that no longer suits one opening may work elsewhere. Sound joinery might need a new layout rather than complete replacement. Condition and suitability should be assessed before committing to reuse.</p><h3>Leave room for discovery</h3><p>Existing buildings rarely reveal everything at once. Allow time for surveys and specialist advice, and keep a contingency for what may be uncovered. Reuse can reduce unnecessary replacement, but careful removal, storage, and adaptation still require planning and a realistic budget.</p><div class=\"takeaway\"><strong>A useful first step</strong><p>Choose five things you would miss if they disappeared. That small list can be the beginning of a more personal renovation brief.</p></div></div></article></div>'''
page('blog_preview.html','Journal','Three complete field notes on natural light, enduring materials, and thoughtful reuse in architecture.',journal)
print('Created work and journal pages.')

